1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,710
Can empty space make something from nothing?

2
00:00:04,175 --> 00:00:06,500
Empty space is never truly empty.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,445
Pairs of particles flicker into being, and vanish again.

4
00:00:11,445 --> 00:00:14,969
But at the edge of a black hole, one of the pair can fall in,

5
00:00:15,269 --> 00:00:16,655
while the other escapes.

6
00:00:17,655 --> 00:00:20,817
That glow is real: Hawking radiation,

7
00:00:20,817 --> 00:00:25,005
predicted in 1974, but still too faint to detect.

8
00:00:26,005 --> 00:00:27,177
But it isn't free.

9
00:00:27,577 --> 00:00:31,875
Each escaping particle is paid for with the black hole's own mass.

10
00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:38,528
A black hole as heavy as the Sun would need 10⁶⁷ years to evaporate.

11
00:00:38,828 --> 00:00:42,485
The universe is just 13.8 billion years old.

12
00:00:43,485 --> 00:00:47,405
So even at the edge of a black hole, nothing is really free.

